FOLLOW-UP FIX: PER-USER REQUEST NUMBERING
---------------------------------------------
Issue raised: a new user's first-ever request showed as "#4" (or
whatever the next global database row happened to be), which looked
confusing - a user with only one request expects to see "#1", not an
arbitrary large number.

Root cause: the database id is a single global sequence shared across
ALL users (correct and necessary for uniqueness), but the UI was
displaying that raw id directly to the user.

Fix: added a per-user "display number", computed on the frontend only.
Each user's own requests are sorted by creation date and numbered 1, 2,
3... independently of the underlying database id. The real database id
is still used internally for URLs and admin actions - only what the
USER SEES on screen changed. Admins still see the real id, since they
are viewing every user's requests mixed together, where per-user
numbering would not make sense.

Also fixed in the same pass: the workload-level radio buttons were
rendering in the browser's default blue instead of the site's teal
accent color - fixed with an accent-color CSS property.

Applied consistently to both the Dashboard table and the Request
Detail page header.

